From 42320a35fbc4333e15ce5b1858a97fd0499cee42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NBG-TOPGEE <NBG-TOPGEE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:49:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Integrate restaurant discovery with Spring Boot backend

- src/api/restaurants.ts: getRestaurants()/getRestaurant() now call the
  real GET /api/restaurants (unwraps items/total/page/limit/pages) and
  GET /api/restaurants/{id} (unwraps restaurant/menu/reviewCount).
  Removed getFeaturedRestaurants(), which hit a nonexistent
  /restaurants/featured endpoint and had no callers.
- src/api/menu.ts: getMenu() calls the real
  GET /api/restaurants/{id}/menu. Removed getMenuItem(), which hit a
  nonexistent single-item endpoint and had no callers. Added
  getMenuItemCategories() for GET /api/categories (menu-item category
  counts) for future use - see note below.
- src/hooks/queries.ts: restaurantsQuery/restaurantQuery/menuQuery now
  call the real API instead of filtering mock data. categoriesQuery,
  popularItemsQuery, ordersQuery and addressesQuery are unchanged
  (mock) - later phases.

Backend limitations documented in code comments rather than papered
over with invented data:
- Restaurant has one cuisine string, not the multi-tag category
  taxonomy the discovery chips use, and no per-restaurant minOrder or
  distanceKm (no geolocation in this app). The category filter is
  passed through as a best-effort 'cuisine' query param, minOrder
  defaults to 0, distanceKm to 0.
- deliveryTimeMinutes is a single estimate, not a range - the UI's
  delivery window is approximated as [estimate, estimate+10].
- MenuItem's restaurant relation is @JsonIgnore'd server-side, so
  restaurantId is injected client-side from the query, not read off
  each item.
- Jackson serializes isOpen()/isAvailable() as 'open'/'available'
  (boolean getter naming), not 'isOpen'/'isAvailable' - mappers use
  the real key names.
- GET /api/categories aggregates menu-item categories with counts,
  a different concept from the curated cuisine tiles on the homepage/
  discovery page; left on mock data (mockCategories) rather than
  wired to a mismatched endpoint.

tsc --noEmit and npm run build both pass; the one remaining tsc error
(orders.local.ts) is pre-existing on main and unrelated.
